Proposed Guiding Question Order
Part I — How LLMs Work

1. What is a token, and why do LLMs represent text in token form?

2. What is the difference between training and inference in an LLM?

3. What are the limits of in-context learning?

4. What is temperature, and how does it affect the variability of generated text?

5. What is retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), and how does it change answers?

6. What is alignment, and how is it related to safer text generation?



Part II — Understanding LLM Reliability

7. What are hallucinations in LLM text generation?

8. Why do hallucinations happen even when the prompt seems specific?

9. How might hallucinated citations mislead research and create downstream harm?

10. What ethical harms can occur when LLMs mass-produce "evidence" summaries that look credible but are wrong?



Part III — LLMs and Cybersecurity

11. How can prompt injection be ethically relevant to safety and integrity in real deployments?

12. What ethical risks arise when LLMs contribute to phishing or fraud at scale?

13. How can LLMs enable coordinated inauthentic behavior at scale through generated text?



Part IV — Bias, Persuasion, and Automated Decisions

14. How can dataset selection and labeling practices make surveillance tools systematically unfair?

15. How can biased language and stereotyping in outputs create real-world harm?

16. What ethical issues arise when businesses use LLMs for persuasion and targeted messaging?

17. What ethical risks arise when LLM outputs are used for hiring or employee evaluation?

18. What ethical issues arise when "extremism" or "dissidence" labels reflect political priorities rather than neutral criteria?




Part V — AI and Mass Surveillance

19. What is mass surveillance in the AI context?

20. How can text generation models support mass surveillance workflows?

21. What ethical concerns arise in workplace surveillance via LLM analysis of employee messages?

22. What ethical questions should guide proportionality when surveillance is used at population scale?



Part VI — Responsible Human Use of AI

23. What principles should guide the use of LLMs as learning aids rather than replacements for human authorship and work?
